===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun|head=[[art|Art]] [[Deco]]}}

# {{lb|en|architecture|art|also|attributive}} A [[style#Noun|style]] of [[decorative#Adjective|decorative]] [[art#Noun|art]] (often [[use#Verb|used]] in [[household#Noun|household]] [[object#Noun|objects]]) and [[architecture]] [[originate|originating]] in the 1920s and 1930s, [[characterize]]d by [[bold#Adjective|bold]] [[geometric]] [[form#Noun|forms]], [[strong#Adjective|strong]] [[contrasting#Adjective|contrasting]] [[color#Noun|colours]], and [[simple#Adjective|simple]] [[composition]]. {{defdate|from 1966}}
#: {{synonyms|en|Deco}}
#* {{RQ:FT|date=26 July 1978|author=Paula Judge|title=Saleroom: {{w|Jane Austen}} Ms {{quote-gloss|manuscript}} sold for £38,000|issue=27,620|page=5|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/FinancialTimes1978UKEnglish/Jul%2026%201978%252C%20Financial%20Times%252C%20%252327620%252C%20UK%20(en)/page/n4/mode/1up|column=8|passage=A pair of Armada flagons were bought for £1,900 and a four-piece '''Art Deco''' tea set on a matching tray was sold at £1,500.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1989|chapter=Introduction|title=National Register of Historic Places 1966–1988|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:State Historic Preservation Office#NCSHPO|National Conference of State Historic Preservation Officers]]; {{w|National Park Service}}; Nashville, Tenn.: {{w|American Association for State and Local History}}|page=ix|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/nationalregister0000unse/page/n10/mode/1up|column=1|isbn=978-0-942063-03-5|passage=These properties reflect the wide variety of resources that represent the American heritage. They include skyscrapers in Chicago and mining structures in Alaska; prehistoric petroglyphs in Nevada and World War II fortifications in Guam; a district of '''art deco''' buildings in Miami and a district of prominent artists' and writers' homes in Santa Fe.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author=Rosanna Cirigliano|chapter=Los Angeles|title=California|location=Florence|publisher=Casa Editrice Bonechi; San Francisco, Calif.: Smith Novelty Company|page=119|pageurl=https://books.google.com.sg/books?id=rmAY3cqpOEgC&pg=PA119|isbn=978-0-938765-30-1|passage=Always in the same district, at 61 Olive Street, is the ''Oviatt Building'', one of {{quote-gloss|the}} most important examples of '''Art Deco''' architecture found within Los Angeles city limits, and once a prestigious clothing shop boasting such clients as {{w|Rudolph Valentino}} and {{w|Douglas Fairbanks Jr.}} {{...}} The store was replaced by today's ''Rex II Restaurant'', decorated as an{{sic|a}} 1920s ocean liner in keeping with the overall '''Art Deco''' decor.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=15 December 2021|author=Robin Leleux|title=Awards Honour the Best Restoration Projects: The Great Western Railway Craft Skills Award: Victoria Arcade|journal=[[w:Rail (magazine)|Rail]]|location=Peterborough, Cambridgeshire|publisher=[[w:Bauer Media Group|Bauer Media]]|issue=946|page=59|issn=0953-4563|oclc=999467860|passage=This project has stripped away modern PVCu and aluminium shop fronts to reveal original or 1930s '''Art Deco''' work, with judicious repairs and replacements as necessary, plus stonework repairs and the restoration of traditional London Transport signage.}}

